s-warren.jpg","pageCount":496,"summary":"\"In 1890, on Indian reservations across the West, followers of a new religion danced in circles until they collapsed into trances. In an attempt to suppress this new faith, the US Army killed over two hundred Lakota Sioux at Wounded Knee Creek. Louis Warren's God's Red Son offers a startling new view of the religion known as the Ghost Dance, from its origins in the visions of a Northern Paiute named Wovoka to the tragedy in South Dakota. To this day, the Ghost Dance remains widely mischaracterized as a primitive and failed effort by Indian militants to resist American conquest and return to traditional ways. In fact, followers of the Ghost Dance sought to thrive in modern America by working for wages, farming the land, and educating their children, tenets that helped the religion endure for decades after Wounded Knee.","readerProfile":{"readerLevel":"crossover","narrativeScore":0.132,"accessibilityScore":0.041,"scholarlyScore":0.074,"traits":[{"id":"narrative","score":0.2,"confidence":"low","evidence":["catalog text contains \"quest\""]},{"id":"character_driven","score":0.18,"confidence":"low","evidence":["catalog text contains \"family\""]}],"method":"deterministic_catalog_heuristics_v1"},"experimentalSemanticProfile":{"centralFigures":[],"centralPlaces":[{"name":"Wounded Knee Creek","confidence":0.78}],"argument":{"present":true,"statement":"Contrary to the view that the Ghost Dance was a failed resistance, its followers sought to thrive in modern America through wages, farming, and educating children, enabling the religion to endure after Wounded Knee.","confidence":0.6},"academicOrientation":{"score":65,"confidence":0.55},"profileConfidence":0.74,"lowerConfidenceCandidates":{"centralFigures":[{"name":"Wovoka","confidence":0.62}],"centralPlaces":[{"name":"South Dakota","confidence":0.55}]},"model":"gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17","promptVersion":8,"inputHash":"e889962be1b64f2e4bfcd8250e8aa11415623c0d375e8c81b1e7b7edc4d7ab3f","re